---
import DemoShell from '../DemoShell.astro';
import Control from './Control.astro';
---

<DemoShell
  id="demo-dendrite"
  code="D3"
  title="Phase-field dendrite"
  poster="d3-dendrite.png"
  alt="Metal dendrites in yellow and green growing upward from a flat electrode into purple electrolyte, computed by the phase-field model"
  aspect="16/9"
  caption="Metal (yellow) plating upward from a flat electrode into electrolyte (purple), running live as a fragment shader. The run starts a few thousand steps in, so the deposit is already visible; reseed to watch it from the flat electrode."
  presets={[
    { label: 'Low drive: compact, mossy growth', state: { 'd3-morph': 15, 'd3-eta': 90 } },
    { label: 'Six-fold crystal: different branch angles', state: { 'd3-omega': 6, 'd3-delta': 0.025 } },
    { label: 'No anisotropy: δ = 0', state: { 'd3-delta': 0 } },
    { label: 'Show the depleted electrolyte', state: { 'd3-showc': 1 } },
  ]}
>
  <fieldset class="panel" slot="panel" disabled>
    <legend>deposition cell</legend>
    {/* The physical overpotential a potentiostat would apply, as a positive
        magnitude so the slider is monotonic. The sign is applied once, and the
        dimensionless aη is derived from it, in d3-dendrite.ts. */}
    <Control id="d3-eta" label="cathodic |η| / mV" min={50} max={205} value={154} step={1} />
    <Control id="d3-delta" label="anisotropy δ" min={0} max={0.06} value={0.051} step={0.001} />
    {/* Morphology stays slider-only: its readout is a regime name, and a
        typed L_η would be a number most readers cannot place. */}
    <div class="control-row">
      <label for="d3-morph">morphology L_σ/L_η</label>
      <input type="range" id="d3-morph" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="75" />
      <output class="readout" id="d3-morph-out" for="d3-morph">dendritic</output>
    </div>
    <Control id="d3-T" label="temperature / K" min={250} max={350} value={298} step={1} />
    <Control id="d3-nuc" label="nucleation sites" min={1} max={8} value={3} step={1} />
    <div class="switch-row" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Anisotropy mode">
      <label><input type="radio" name="d3-omega" value="4" checked /> ω = 4</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="d3-omega" value="6" /> ω = 6</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="d3-showc" /> concentration</label>
    </div>
    <div class="switch-row">
      <button class="tool-btn" type="button" id="d3-pause" aria-pressed="false">pause</button>
      <button class="tool-btn" type="button" id="d3-reset">reseed from flat</button>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-readouts">
      <span>aη = Fη/RT <output id="d3-aeta">—</output></span>
      <span>solid fraction <output id="d3-solid">—</output></span>
      <span>interface / width <output id="d3-rough">—</output></span>
      <span>rate <output id="d3-rate">—</output></span>
      <span>backend <output id="d3-backend">—</output></span>
      <span id="d3-stability-note" role="status" hidden
        >⚠ <output id="d3-stability">—</output></span
      >
    </div>
  </fieldset>
  <Fragment slot="notes">
    <p>
      An electrochemically driven Allen–Cahn phase field with Kobayashi anisotropy. ξ = 1 is metal
      and ξ = 0 electrolyte; the ion concentration co-evolves and throttles growth where the
      electrolyte depletes. It runs as a WebGL2 fragment shader on ping-pong float textures, with a
      CPU fallback at reduced resolution.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Length and time are nondimensional lattice units. The picture is a morphology, not a
        prediction of micrometres or seconds.
      </li>
      <li>
        The morphology control moves the L<sub>σ</sub>/L<sub>η</sub> ratio through the sequence
        mossy → needle → dendritic.
      </li>
      <li>
        The overpotential control is the physical cathodic overpotential as a magnitude in
        millivolts, which is what a potentiostat applies: drag right for more drive. The group that
        enters the Butler–Volmer exponentials, aη = Fη/RT, is derived from it and shown in the
        readouts. It carries T in its denominator, so it moves with temperature even though the
        applied millivolts do not.
      </li>
      <li>
        Explicit time stepping has a stability limit. If a setting crosses it, the readouts say so
        rather than letting an unstable picture pass as a solution.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</DemoShell>

<script>
  import { initWhenVisible } from './runtime';
  initWhenVisible('demo-dendrite', async (shell) => {
    const { init } = await import('./d3-dendrite');
    init(shell);
  });
</script>
